        q: "Which occupations are not eligible for the BC PNP?",
        a: "For applications submitted after June 13, 2026, section 3.11 of the program guide makes 12 occupations ineligible in every Skills Immigration stream: executive assistants (12100), human resources and recruitment officers (12101), accounting technicians and bookkeepers (12200), administrative officers (13100), administrative assistants (13110), legal administrative assistants (13111), medical administrative assistants (13112), retail sales supervisors (62010), food service supervisors (62020), real estate agents and salespersons (63101), religious leaders (41302) and religious workers (42204). TEER 4 and 5 jobs are also out, because the Skilled Worker stream accepts only TEER 0 to 3 and the Entry Level and Semi-Skilled stream is closed.",
        links: [{ href: "/articles/bc-pnp-ineligible-occupations-employers-2027", label: "Ineligible occupations and employers" }],
      },
      {
        q: "What documents are required for the BC PNP from the employer?",
        a: "Employer documents are uploaded with your application after you are invited, and the 30-day deadline makes it wise to prepare them early. Expect a signed BC PNP employer declaration form; a signed job offer letter showing the job title, duties, wage, hours, full-time indeterminate terms and work location; proof the business is established and in good standing in B.C. and meets the employee minimum; and, where required, evidence of recruitment. You also need reference letters from current and past employers that prove your experience, with duties, dates, hours and pay. The BC PNP may contact your employer to verify the offer, so make sure someone there will respond.",
        links: [
          { href: "/articles/bc-pnp-documents-checklist", label: "BC PNP documents checklist" },
          { href: "/articles/bc-pnp-employer-guide", label: "BC PNP employer guide" },
        ],
      },
    ],
