      {
        q: "Can health care workers apply through the Health Authority stream?",
        a: "Yes. The Health Authority stream is for people employed or offered a job by one of B.C.'s public health authorities, mainly in health occupations (any NOC starting with 3) plus a few social and community service roles; physicians, nurse practitioners and midwives supported by a health authority have their own rules. Unlike the Skilled Worker stream, you apply directly, without registering or waiting for a draw. Health workers with private employers can register under the Skilled Worker stream and compete in Care: Health draws, which had a minimum of 76 points in September and October 2026. In 2025, 1,678 people were nominated through the Health Authority stream.",
        links: [{ href: "/articles/bc-pnp-health-authority-stream", label: "Health Authority stream guide" }],
      },
      {
        q: "Can I get BC PNP as an entrepreneur?",
        a: "Yes, through Entrepreneur Immigration, which does not need a job offer. You register and are scored; if invited, you apply, sign a performance agreement and come to B.C. on a work permit to establish and actively manage the business. Nomination for permanent residence follows once you meet the agreement. There is a Base stream and a Regional stream for smaller communities, which requires a referral from a participating community. Draws are small: on September 22, 2026, the Base round invited 10 people at a minimum score of 119. Fees are $300 to register and $3,500 to apply. Check net worth and investment minimums in the current Entrepreneur Immigration guide.",
        links: [{ href: "/articles/bc-pnp-entrepreneur-immigration", label: "Entrepreneur Immigration guide" }],
      },
      {
        q: "Can my spouse and children come with me through the BC PNP?",
        a: "Yes. The BC PNP nominates you as the principal applicant, and your spouse or common-law partner and dependent children can be included in your permanent residence application to IRCC and become permanent residents with you. Under IRCC's rules, a dependent child is generally under 22 and not a spouse or partner, or older and dependent because of a physical or mental condition. Each family member adds IRCC fees: $1,590 for a spouse or partner and $270 per child. Declare every family member accurately in both your BC PNP and IRCC applications, including anyone who will not move with you.",
        links: [{ href: "/articles/bc-pnp-spouse-family-guide-2026", label: "Spouse and family guide" }],
      },
      {
        q: "What is PNP-BC (medical)?",
        a: "It has nothing to do with the BC PNP. In U.S. nursing, PNP means pediatric nurse practitioner, and the -BC (board certified) suffix belongs to credentials from the American Nurses Credentialing Center, such as its PPCNP-BC, whose initial certification ANCC retired after 2019. New pediatric nurse practitioners now certify through the Pediatric Nursing Certification Board as CPNP-PC or CPNP-AC.",
        links: [{ href: "/articles/bc-pnp-nurses-health-care-assistants-2027", label: "Nurses and the BC PNP" }],
      },
    ],
  },
];

const allItems = groups.flatMap((g) => g.items);

const faqSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": allItems.map((f) => ({
    "@type": "Question",
    "name": f.q,
    "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": f.a,
    },
  })),
};
